  "assessment": {
    "cause": "The existing shift/OR formulas differ from hashes of equal JDK reflection objects, allowing equal Type values to occupy different hash buckets.",
    "implementation": "Generic arrays return the component hash. Parameterized types combine the type-argument array, nullable owner and raw-type hashes using XOR. Nested type composition therefore uses the same compatible component results.",
    "invariants": [
      "Generic array component and parameterized raw type are validated non-null by existing factories. Parameterized type arguments reject null elements; a nullable owner is handled by Objects.hashCode.",
      "No public signature, constructor, equality, wildcard normalization, type traversal, changelog, dependency or workflow behavior changes. Reversal of the two edited method bodies reproduces the entire upstream source file.",
      "Tests use public reflection, no internal JDK classes or fixed numeric hash expectations. They establish symmetric equality before checking exact hash equality and HashMap/HashSet lookup in both directions.",
      "The 14 cases cover top-level parameterized types, raw/static owners, parameterized owners with generic and non-generic members, nested arguments, type variables, one- and two-dimensional generic arrays and wildcard/array composition."
    ],
    "runtime_result_interpretation": "Every baseline case reached assertAll after both equality assertions and failed all five hash/collection checks. Every corresponding new case passed in the fixed whole-class run without skips. The one fixed-run skip is an unchanged existing TODO-disabled method.",
    "jdk_specification_limit": "Java SE interface documentation does not prescribe these exact hash algorithms. This correction establishes observed JDK reflection interoperability, not a newly specified Java SE algorithm. Other supported runtime/platform results remain to be observed."
  },
